  "result": "Fixed. The quote now prints **27.06**.\n\n**Root cause:** the bug was in `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9`, which turns a price string into whole cents. It padded the decimal part on the wrong side: `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` turned `\"12.5\"` into `\"05\"`, so the price became 1205 cents ($12.05) instead of 1250. Two of those plus 8.25% tax gave 26.09. Any price with one decimal place was affected.\n\n**Fix:** pad on the right instead (`frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`), so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`. I also removed `or 0`, which can no longer do anything. I checked these inputs by hand: `12` \u2192 1200, `12.` \u2192 1200, `12.5` \u2192 1250, `12.05` \u2192 1205, `0.5` \u2192 50. With the right price, 2 \u00d7 1250 = 2500 cents, plus 206 cents tax, gives 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`, and all 6 pass. None of them test `to_cents`, which is why this got through. I can add a small regression test for one-decimal prices if you'd like.",
